    {
      "text": "{\"type\":\"final_answer\",\"content\":\"All steps are complete. Here's a summary with one important caveat:\\n\\n**Completed ✅**\\n1. **Living room scan** — 4 devices found: `lock_main`, `light_living`, `ac_living`, `tv_living`.\\n2. **`light_living` status** — was off at 0% brightness, white.\\n3. **Lock** — `lock_main` was online, powered on, and already locked. It was set to `locked` using PIN 2026; the tool confirmed success (`new_state: \\\"locked\\\"`).\\n4. **Notification** — sent with title \\\"Untitled\\\" to user_123.\\n\\n**Caveat: the light is NOT at 30% brightness.**\\nMy first attempt to turn the light on at 30% returned a **failed** result (it reported `device_id: light_bedroom`, a mismatch, and `executed_failed`). I did not assume it was working. Instead I used the two fallback tools that actually applied to `light_living`:\\n- `set_power_state` → on ✅\\n- `update_device_config` (brightness) → 30 ✅\\n- `update_device_config` (color) → white ✅\\n\\nSo the light is now on, white, at 30% brightness. However, this relied on the fallback path rather than the primary light API, so if the device's state metadata still shows \\\"off\\\" or \\\"0%\\\" in its status feed, please verify with the light's own status endpoint.\"}\n</action>",
      "model": true,
